#{[17592186435414 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435414 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70482" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435414 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435414 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a9/a9278747563bf79a79e5633f2fae6d97a6ba021c38e8238ef9d22d1396df0be6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435414 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8116a58f-3c71-50dd-834a-34d7c8e130b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435414 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-2,-2),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=4,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
